# Architecture and State

- Follow the project's feature and package boundaries. In a new app, group related UI, state, and data by feature. Put genuinely shared primitives and services in the shared layer.
- Keep routes and screens focused on composition. Views render state and dispatch intents. Controllers or use cases coordinate operations. Repositories and adapters own I/O and translate transport models and errors at the boundary.
- Use the project's established state model (BLoC/Cubit, Riverpod, signals, native toolkit state, or equivalent). Give each piece of state one owner. Inject dependencies where doing so makes boundaries testable.
- Model initial loading, ready, empty, recoverable error, and stale data as needed. Keep mutation state scoped by action or entity. Trigger navigation and feedback once per outcome.
- Retain valid data during refresh. Invalidate or update affected state after a child route or dialog commits a change. Use the existing shared state or route result instead of adding a parallel cache.
- Scope subscriptions and renders to the data consumed by each component. Preserve list identity, selection, and scroll when unrelated state changes.
- For repeated intents, choose the intended behavior explicitly. Deduplicate identical requests, coalesce superseded intents, or queue distinct operations when order matters. Protect newer state from late responses and older rollbacks.
